// Intake for 2026-09-18 — their words, my translation, the triage verdict (D0166/D0216).
// Written by `keel record statement` / `keel record story` (D0236): a Statement's text is
// VERBATIM, so it is escaped for the literal and otherwise untouched.
package ProjectIntake20260918 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineIntake::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    part st160 : Statement {
        :>> id = "241c5ac5-f188-4703-84ea-57f0ede3cf16";
        :>> title = "GH#85 [bug] Two packages may reuse a part name: every gate stays green, a typed edge binds to the wrong item, and a qualified reference does not parse";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "Two packages may declare the same part name. Nothing detects it, a typed edge then binds to the wrong item **silently**, and there is no syntax to disambiguate because a qualified reference does not parse. Found in a downstream project (plotwright), where two `#DerivedFrom` edges are already pointing at the wrong UserStory and a guard's prescribed fix cannot be written at all.\n\nBinary: `keel 0.4.1`, build `444333d`.\n\n## Minimal repro, on a tree freshly created by this binary\n\n`keel init`, then two files under `.tracking/intake/`:\n\n`alpha.sysml`\n```\npackage IntakeAlpha {\n    private import EngineElement::*;\n    private import EngineIntake::*;\n    private import EngineRelationships::*;\n\n    part st001 : Statement {\n        :>> id = ''11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111'';\n        :>> title = ''ALPHA - the statement the edge below actually means'';\n        ...\n    }\n\n    part us001 : UserStory {\n        :>> id = ''33333333-3333-4333-8333-333333333333'';\n        :>> title = ''ALPHA story - derived from the ALPHA statement'';\n        ...\n    }\n    #DerivedFrom dependency from us001 to st001;\n}\n```\n\n`beta.sysml` declares a **different** Statement, different UUID, reusing the part name:\n```\npackage IntakeBeta {\n    part st001 : Statement {\n        :>> id = ''22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222'';\n        :>> title = ''BETA - a DIFFERENT statement that happens to reuse the part name st001'';\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNote the edge is written **inside `IntakeAlpha`**, which declares its own `st001` three lines above it.\n\n### Result\n\n```\n$ keel gate validate .\n3 tracking file(s) validated clean.\n\n$ keel gate guard .\n[guard:duplicate-identity] PASS — 608 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s)\n[guard:type-collision]     PASS — 0 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s)\n\n$ keel show why us001\n  ''element'': ''us001'',\n  ''title'':   ''ALPHA story - derived from the ALPHA statement'',\n  ''file'':    ''.tracking/intake/alpha.sysml'',\n\n  ''element'': ''st001'',\n  ''title'':   ''BETA - a DIFFERENT statement that happens to reuse the part name st001'',\n  ''via'':     ''us001 ->derivedfrom st001'',\n  ''file'':    ''.tracking/intake/beta.sysml'',\n```\n\nThe edge reaches **beta**. A local declaration in the edge's own package does not win, and every gate is green while the model asserts a provenance link that is false.\n\n## Why this is severe rather than untidy\n\n`duplicate-identity` passes because it compares UUIDs, and the UUIDs genuinely differ. So the one control whose name suggests it covers this is looking at a different axis. Meanwhile `CLAUDE.md` invariant 3 states plainly that *''Every item has an immutable `id` (UUID); `title` is a human string; `displayLabel` is computed. Items never collide on name.''* That invariant is asserted in the contract and enforced nowhere.\n\nThe failure is silent in the worst way: the tree is green, the edge exists, `trace`/`why`/coverage all report confidently, and the only symptom is that the wrong item is named.\n\n## There is no way to write the disambiguation\n\nThe obvious fix at the authoring site is a qualified reference. The parser rejects it:\n\n```\n#DerivedFrom dependency from d0010 to ProjectIntake20260917::st022;\n```\n```\nPARSE: 0010-recommendationIsTheCourse.sysml:26:64: expected Semicolon, got ColonColon\n```\n\nSo an author who *notices* the collision still cannot express which item they mean. The only remedy available today is renaming one of the colliding parts, which is a bulk migration across every file that references it.\n\n(This is adjacent to #72, which reports the parser refusing a *named* dependency. Same area, different clause: this one is the reference target, not the dependency's own identification.)\n\n## What it did to a real project\n\nIn plotwright, `.tracking/intake/intake-2026-09-18.sysml` restarts Statement and UserStory numbering at `st022`/`us069` and collides with `intake-2026-09-17.sysml` on **eleven** names — `st022`–`st025` and `us069`–`us075`. The file's own header documents renumbering the three colliding *Decisions* on merge under D0022; the Statements and UserStories were missed, and nothing said so.\n\nTwo edges are already mis-bound, in `.tracking/business/plotwright-beat-facets.sysml` lines 90 and 91:\n\n| Edge | Binds to | Means |\n|---|---|---|\n| `srPwBeatFacets -> us071` | 09-18 ''Each stage page carries the feedback relevant to that stage'' | 09-17 ''The beat facets live in the tool's schema, not in a process document'' |\n| `srPwDeedRatio -> us072` | 09-18 ''Is there enough material: beats per character per act'' | 09-17 ''The deed ratio is a computed reading, not a rule of thumb'' |\n\nWorse, it makes another guard's instruction impossible to follow. `guard:direction-cited` fails d0010 with:\n\n> `d0010: quotes the human and no #DerivedFrom edge to a Statement ... Record their words with keel record statement and author #DerivedFrom dependency from d0010 to <stNNN>;`\n\nThe Statement holding the quoted span exists — `st022`, id `11829d9e-43e5-470f-9691-5f6b670b5e18`, at `intake-2026-09-17.sysml:61`. Authoring exactly the prescribed edge changed the complaint to *''no linked Statement's text holds this span verbatim''*, because it bound to the 09-18 `st022` instead. The edge was reverted rather than left in place: a link to the wrong Statement is a false provenance claim and worse than a missing one. So `direction-cited` currently cannot be discharged by any edge the grammar accepts.\n\nThere is a sharp irony in that pairing. `direction-cited` exists (issue428/D0463, hazard EHZ3) so the tree holds the human's real words rather than the agent's rendering of them — and the resolution mechanism it mandates can quietly attach those words to the wrong statement.\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n1. **A guard that fails on a duplicate part name across the model**, sitting beside `duplicate-identity` and checking the name axis rather than the UUID axis. This is the smallest fix and it turns a silent mis-binding into a loud one.\n2. **Make an ambiguous reference an error rather than a silent pick.** If two declarations match a bare name, the edge should refuse to resolve and name both candidates with their files, instead of choosing.\n3. **Accept a qualified reference in an edge target** (`Package::name`), so an author who has a legitimate reason to keep both names has a way to say which one they mean. Without at least one of 2 or 3, noticing the bug does not help you fix it.\n4. **Cover Statements and UserStories at merge time the way Decisions already are.** Worth being precise about where the eleven collisions came from, because it is not the minting path: `keel record statement` allocates globally today — run in this same project a few minutes ago it produced `st036`, correctly above the existing high-water mark of `st035`. The collisions came from two branches each minting from their own view and then merging. That is exactly the case Decisions have handling for (this project's D0022 records the three Decisions being renumbered on merge, and #30 covered the engine-reference side), and Statements and UserStories have none. A merge that silently produces eleven duplicate names is the upstream cause; items 1–3 are what stops it being silent.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/85";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st161 : Statement {
        :>> id = "43209b75-477f-4d2a-8fc8-4637fda75ade";
        :>> title = "GH#84 decision-surfacing dsGround binds checkedBy to judgment-request-quality, which never reads the rendered decision page";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "Found in the Penumbra project (asirobots/Penumbra) on 2026-09-18 after its owner could not see the bifurcation on a six-tab decision page and a five-critic panel read the tabs against the rules that govern them.\n\n**What the process says.** `processes/decision-surfacing.sysml`, step `dsGround` (''Give them what an INDEPENDENT judgment needs, not a request to bless my pick'') demands the item itself, what accepting binds, the fork and the evidence inline, and declares `checkedBy = ''judgment-request-quality''`.\n\n**What the check reads.** Per `docs/guards.md`, `judgment-request-quality` scans a PROPOSED fork Decision's title, rationale, RESEARCH statement and per-OPTION cost in the decisions directory. It does not read the rendered exec-summary page or the project's tab file, and `scripts/check_templates.py` is structural only (word budgets, field presence, chain order). So the step promises enforcement of a content property and nothing enforces it.\n\n**What got through** (six tabs, one page): two tabs whose options differed only by a verdict verb (''Judge ... as PASS'' / ''Fail ... with a direction''); one option whose cost read literally `None.` (the exec-summary skill's own text says never ''none''); before/after rows whose both cells were record states (''PROPOSED'' -> ''judged''); a computed classification preamble rendered before every tab. The skill forbade three of the four; nothing read the tab.\n\n**Proposed fix, either of:**\n\n1. Retarget `checkedBy` to a check that reads the rendered page. The shapes are structural enough for `check_templates.py`: a `data-d=''cost''` whose text is exactly none, n/a or a dash; a choice label starting with a verdict verb (accept, reject, approve, confirm, pass, fail, judge); a before/after table row whose two cells are both drawn from a closed record-state vocabulary; and, if you take the field, a required `data-d=''premise''` rendered before the choices, budgeted like `stake`.\n2. Or declare the step unenforceable with a reason, per D0321's own convention, so the process stops claiming a control it does not have.\n\n**Reference implementation.** Penumbra's project-side floor is `tools/tab_quality.py` on branch `sitting-2026-09-17-attestations` (its `--self-test` holds the two offending tabs as the shapes that must refuse, and a premise-led tab and a hand-only tab as the shapes that must pass); the decision recording the panel's findings is that project's D1132.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/84";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part us115 : UserStory {
        :>> id = "13c81de1-0cba-4003-a985-25a9c5d5c7b3";
        :>> title = "A declared name is unique across the model, and a bare reference two declarations match refuses";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> asA = "keel adopter authoring typed edges across packages";
        :>> iWant = "a second declaration of a part name anywhere in the model to fail the tree loudly, and a bare reference that two declarations match to refuse naming both with their files, instead of a typed edge silently binding to whichever declaration the model read last";
        :>> soThat = "a green tree never asserts a provenance edge to the wrong item";
        :>> implication = ImplicationKind::bug;
        :>> triageNote = "Verified against this tree before triage, not taken from the report. members/keel-model/src/model.rs:70 declares `items: HashMap<String, ItemInfo>` keyed by the bare declared name, and the build at model.rs:244 does `items.insert(name, info)` - a second declaration of the same name in another package REPLACES the first, so every edge naming it binds to whichever file the walk read last. members/keel-guards/src/identity.rs:309 scopes the duplicate-name check to `within one package` (the violation text at :389 says `in package`), so the reported cross-package case passes duplicate-identity exactly as described; CLAUDE.md invariant 3 (`Items never collide on name`) is asserted and enforced by nothing. UNTRUSTED source (public repo, D0264): plan only. The reporter's fixes 1 (a name guard on the name axis), 2 (an ambiguous bare reference refuses naming both candidates) and 4 (the silence at merge time) are ONE defect - the model's identity key is the bare name and nothing holds it unique - routed to one Issue and one proposed Decision; fix 3 (a qualified `Package::name` target) is its own story. Fix 4's automation (renumbering Statements and UserStories on merge the way Decisions were) is not adopted here: once the name is held unique model-wide, a merge that lands two declarations goes red at the land gate naming both files, and the rename across references is the author's migration (D0067), not a silent renumber. Severity High: the tree stays green while `why`, `trace` and coverage assert a false provenance link, and a guard's prescribed fix (direction-cited) becomes impossible to discharge.";
    }
    #DerivedFrom dependency from us115 to st160;

    part us116 : UserStory {
        :>> id = "d4f7ecf6-a702-4ae6-ab9b-2e74d0627244";
        :>> title = "A qualified Package::name is accepted as the target of a typed edge";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> asA = "keel adopter who has noticed two packages share a part name";
        :>> iWant = "to write a qualified reference (Package::name) as the target of a typed edge, so that when two packages legitimately keep the same part name I can say which one I mean instead of renaming one of them across every file that references it";
        :>> soThat = "noticing the collision gives me a way to say which item I mean";
        :>> implication = ImplicationKind::bug;
        :>> triageNote = "The reporter's fix 3, split from the defect because it is the way out the control then requires, not the control. The parse refusal is real (the report quotes `expected Semicolon, got ColonColon` on 0.4.1) and the OMG grammar allows a qualified name there. First triaged as declined on the premise that names would be made unique model-wide; the measurement that followed (271 declared names duplicated across this tree's own .tracking and .engine, 247 of them sprint gate names reused across sprint packages by design) showed that premise renames history for a property the language does not require, so the recommended course became D0517 OPTION A: a bare reference resolves locally, else uniquely, else REFUSES naming every candidate - and the author that refusal stops needs exactly this qualified target to say which item they mean. Adopted as part of OPTION A; declined only if the human chooses OPTION B (model-wide unique names, a 271-name migration). The parser lane is GH#72's (a named dependency: the sibling clause of the same grammar rule). Routed with us115 to the same Issue and Decision.";
    }
    #DerivedFrom dependency from us116 to st160;

    part us117 : UserStory {
        :>> id = "2946c31c-ac2e-45a8-9c66-87a4dfcc945e";
        :>> title = "The dsGround step binds to a check that reads the rendered decision page";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> asA = "human judging a decision page built by an agent";
        :>> iWant = "the decision-surfacing step that promises every tab carries the item, what accepting binds, the fork and the evidence to be bound to a check that reads the rendered page, or to say plainly that no check does, so the process stops claiming a control it does not have";
        :>> soThat = "a tab that fails the step's own rules is refused before I see it, or the process admits nothing refuses it";
        :>> implication = ImplicationKind::process;
        :>> triageNote = "Verified in this tree: .engine/processes/decision-surfacing.sysml:49 sets dsGround `checkedBy = 'judgment-request-quality'`, and .engine/docs/guards.md:38 describes that guard as reading a PROPOSED fork Decision's title, rationale, RESEARCH statement and per-OPTION cost in the decisions directory - it never opens the rendered page, and no guard names scripts/check_templates.py (grep of guards.md: none). check_templates.py --brief (check_brief at :264) holds word budgets, field presence and chain order; none of the four content shapes the report names (a cost reading literally none/n-a/dash, a choice label led by a verdict verb, a before/after row whose two cells are both record states, a premise rendered before the choices) is read by anything. So the step binds a content promise to a check of a different artefact: a D0321 binding that resolves (the guard exists) and does not check what the step says. UNTRUSTED source (public repo, D0264): plan only. Implication `process`: the fix is to the process step and its check, routed to one Issue with a resolver and one proposed Decision carrying the reporter's two courses as OPTIONS - retarget the binding to a check that reads the page (the four shapes added to check_templates --brief, which the build script already runs and refuses on, since the page is machine-local and no tree guard can open it), or declare the step unenforceable with a reason per D0321's own convention. The reporter's reference implementation (Penumbra tools/tab_quality.py, its --self-test holding the two offending tabs) is named in the Statement and is the shape the resolver's D0388 pair takes.";
    }
    #DerivedFrom dependency from us117 to st161;

    // Routing (github-intake step 3; D0264 untrusted: every routed story names a Decision among its
    // targets, so guard untrusted-routing holds while the change waits on the human's word).
    #Implicates dependency from us115 to issue605;
